A||N||44||1FST2M6||How much faster?!||||5||
Z||000000||1FST2M6||01-13-2000||03:07 PM||||I have a nitrous kit on my car along with many other mods. Wht jetting has any one else run? I have a .042nitrous and .042 bypass which puts 164.4hp and 243.7lb-ft out at the wheels...(dynojet Dyno 248c) I'd like to know what everyone else is running to see how much higher i can go with out roasting the crank or bending a rod! She has 95k on her now! (86 SE V6 4spd) (and have drained 12 bottles, about 8 months worth on the current set up...)<p>[This message has been edited by 1FST2M6 (edited 01-14-2000).]||||reg||5
Z||000001||mrfiero||01-13-2000||04:39 PM||||I'm no expert on nitrous, but I wouldn't push it any further on an engine with that many miles.  Enjoy what you have right now and when the time comes to do a rebuild then you can go crazy on the nitrous (assuming you "build" the engine to handle it!)   ||||reg||7
Z||000002||2FAST4U||01-13-2000||10:14 PM||||Forget the crank and rods, you'll melt a piston long before you hurt the lower end.||||reg||1
Z||000003||DJRice||01-14-2000||09:02 AM||||Shauns got a NOS kit on his ride, maybe he will spot this message and post a note.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Eric||01-14-2000||01:29 PM||||I was thinking of putting nitrous on my new 3.1.  It seems like it would be a lot easier to deal with than a turbo and if I'm carefull, safer too.  1FST2M6, what other mods do you have?  Did you have to bump up the fuel pressure?  What's the .042nitrous and .042 bypass equivalent to; 50 shot, 70shot?<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000005||1FST2M6||01-14-2000||03:33 PM||||The mods are pretty numerous,still running the stock bottom end, but to list but the basics are, Random Technology 2.5" cat, 2.5" 2 chamber flowmaster, 2.5" single out to 4"tip, K&N E-0890, 3mm Ported throttle body, straight pipe where the resonator box should be (does make a difference, have dyno proof) I'll send ya a full set of specs if ya like (4 pages printed) As for the jetting its actually a 40hp shot... but 130lb-ft of torque! Thanks to lots of Dyno tuning and 55lb-hr (mustang cobra, stock) fuel injectors from RC engineering. Fuel pump from 88 IROC and Stillen fuel pressure riser.(90+ psi all the time) I've been talking with Shaun he too has a nitrous kit but can't seem to get the same #s as I... not really sure why though. I've tried many different things eg. no cat. and such but only lost power nearly 14ft-lbs! that was bad since torque is what gets you going... I'd rather be fast 0-60 than go 190! OH yeah fun fact... if you ever rebuild the stock fuel pressure regulator in the rail, place a penny or nickel under the diaphram, and instant +7-10psi! An old guy (in his 70s)at the machine shop told me that and it actually works!||||reg||1
Z||000006||mwbackus||01-14-2000||05:36 PM||||55 lb/hr injectors??  Could you please explain how you were able to make these work on a mostly stock 2.8?  I was under the impression that the ECM could only compensate for a "small" increase in injector flow rate.<p>[This message has been edited by mwbackus (edited 01-14-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||Eric||01-14-2000||08:48 PM||||That's crazy, 90 psi!  I was under the impression rising the fuel pressure didn't work past 50 psi.  I've got mine turned up to 45 or 46, but I guess the stock injectors couldn't handle any more than that.  Does the engine run rich when the nitrous isn't on?  I could imagine it wouldn't work well at all, but what the heck do I know?  I was thinking of getting a Air Fuel gauge like Shaun installed or will install because I'm just not sure if I increased the pressure enough (I don't have any top end and I do have a GM hipo. cam to boot.).  I'd like to have the car running perfectly before I install nitrous, if I install nitrous, because there isn't much point in 50 extra horsepower 2% of the time, if 98% of the time you can't keep up with the family mini van.<P>I heard Shaun was building up a 3.1 motor for next summer.  I wonder what kind of bottom end he's throwing in that thing.  I've got Federal Mogul hyperutectic pistons (not as good as forged, but better than cast so I'm told).  I wonder what kind of jetting they could handle before falling apart.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Eric||01-14-2000||08:52 PM||||Hey 1FST2M6, if you want to email me those specs I'd really appreciate it.  I'm willing to try anything if the evidence is compelling enough.  Thanks.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Shaun41178||01-15-2000||06:42 AM||||OH MY GOD!! I can't believe I almost missed this post!  The grace of god must have been with me.  Or i just saw the word "Faster" in the subject heading and clicked on it.  That is how you get my attention people, by putting faster or engine stuff in the subject heading.<P>Anyway about the nitrous jetting, I have a .036 nitrous and a .042 bypass.  According to my kit the .036 nitrous jet gives 70 extra hp.  I dyno tested my car with a .032 jet(50hp) and got +44 at the wheels with it.  So it seems pretty accurate, the jet settings that is.  With the .032 jet I had 214 lb ft of peak torque at the wheels which was a gain of around 65 more then without nitrous<P>1fst2M6: Great to see you here.  I am glad you joined up and decided to post here.<P>I think the only thing that is keeping you from roasting the crank or rods is all the extra fuel from the 55# injectors.  I still have the stock injectors and get more hp then you with a much smaller jet.  A .042 jet is equivalant to about a 130 hp shot of nitrous.  I don't know if I have asked you this before but have you tried playing with the size of the bypass jet?  You mihgt get more hp by making this jet larger which in turn will help lean out the fuel a bit by not closing off the fuel regulator as much.  If you have time to dyno test I would throw in a .040(just for learning purposes to see what happens) and then go to a .044 and then a .046 and see what happens to your hp output as well as your torque output.  But watch your air/fuel gauge at the same time to make sure you are not leaning out.  Or better yet, if the dyno shop has a motec meter they can place that up your exhaust pipe to get an air/fuel reading and is also much more accurate then your dashboard gauge.<P>Eric a .036 nitrous jet and .042 bypass is equivalant to a increase of 70 hp from the nitrous.  This is the same combo that I am running now on my stock engine and plan on running with my 3.2 liter.  Can you say 12 second Et's?  I sure can.  Hehe  <P>I plan on running 19# injecotrs with my setup as I expect hp ranges of around 300 at the crank with my nitrous.  I want to be safe too.  I hope my computer will be alright.  I might need a special program but no biggie I guess.  Think I will be alright 2M6 with the 19's and my computer?<P>I am keeping my stock bottom end.  Well rods that is.  I am reusing those and am going with hypereutectic pistons as well.  Crank will be a cast nodular iron like everyone else.  I am not worried about it pooping out on me.  I have confidence in my parts selection.  ERIC:  If my stock cast pistons can handle a 70 shot of nitrous then the hypers can too as they are stronger then the factory cast ones.  Just make sure you aren't running lean or use too much cause that is what kills nitrous engines.  A 70 shot will do just fine and will get you around 260-300 crank hp depending on the output of your 3.2.<P>One quick question.  I got the GM cam with .427 intake and .454 exhaust with a 112 degree separation.  I am sure most of you know what cam I am talking about.  Anyways I have purchased a set of comp cams 1.6 rockers.  Please no speeches about the valve guides as I already know.  But with the 1.6 rockers this will give me the equivalant of .455 intake and .484 exhaust lift.  Anyone know if this will be too much lift for my cam to handle?  I sure hope it is not.  If the computer is able to handle it then that would be awesome power coming from this thing.  I would guess 230 crank when tuned.  Anyone know for sure about the lift thing?  I better know now before I get the engine togethor and find out that way.  I will start a new thread on this too to get more opinions I guess too.  Thanks<BR>Shaun- soon to be powered 12 sec Fiero||||reg||1
Z||000010||Shaun41178||01-15-2000||06:48 AM||||Eric I remember yousaying you live in or near Columbus right?  Yougoing to tha tmid Ohio thing?  I haven't read much about it but thought it might be cool.  I will have to read the thread.  If so I wouldn't mind giving you a ride in "the Beast" come that time as my engine should be done by then and broken in.  Also you have a 3.1 right?  we should hit Norwalk this summer.  See what are cars are capable of.  I would like to see what this will run off the nitrous.  interested at all?||||reg||1
Z||000011||batboy||01-15-2000||09:23 AM||||Shaun, no lectures from me this time. You know all about the speed game, it cost a lot of money and it wears out your engine faster, but it's so <B>FUN</B>.<P>I'd worry the most about valve to piston clearance. There is a method using a bit of clay on top of the pistons. You put on the head, tighten the bolts, assemble the pushrods and rocker arms, turn the engine over (rotate the crank), and then pull the heads back off. Check the compressed clay to see if you have enough clearance. If you are using flattop pistons, you'll probably be ok.<P>Remember I said I wasn't going to lecture, well, I lied. I don't think the 1.6 rockers help much in a high performance engine. They work the best in a stock engine to boast some much need lift. Shaun, I plugged the figures in from your 3.1 you're building and the 1.6 rockers added only 1 HP and 1 ft/lb of TQ over the 1.5 rockers on my Desktop Dyno computer program.<P>Do you want some real performance gains, especially since you're going to be using nitrous? The secret is flow. The heads and exhaust have to flow much better than they do stock. A well done pocket porting job and installing the 1.6" exhaust valves will give you probably 15-20 HP on top end and 7-8 TQ (n/a) if you have headers and free flowing exhaust. You should use new heavy duty valve springs, etc. Head work is expensive, but makes power. With nitrous--even better!<P>NOTE: These power figures I'm quoting are hypothetical, your mileage may vary. Also, these figures are gross not net. Net HP and TQ is what you see at the wheels when you dyno your car and is less than gross (at the engine flywheel) readings.<p>[This message has been edited by batboy (edited 01-15-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000012||Eric||01-15-2000||09:43 AM||||Thanks for the tips, Shaun.  I'm glad there's an option like nitrous out there to have some fun with.  I was considering a turbo, but I think it would have been a little over my head.  The exhaust plumbing would be a nightmare.  I'm also glad to hear you're going to use hyperutectic pistons.  I must admit, I was a little gunshy about them at first, but I've done a little checking around and found they are in a lot more high performance engines than I previously thought. <P>Where are you going to get your 19# injectors from?  I assume you're going to use a higher fuel pressure also.  I bumped my pressure up about six psi, but now I'm not sure it was enough for my non-stock 3.2 (I think that coin trick that 1FST2M6 was talking about could work on adjustable regulators also to give even more pressure).  It has super torque up till 5k then I just runs out.  I was at least expecting it to run strong up to 6000, but I seems to just fade away in the power department.  The GT also has the stock ignition and coil (I guess the stock coil really doesn't do it).  What kind of ignition/coil setup are you NO2 guys running?  Oh yeah, do you have to retard the timing a bunch too?||||reg||1
Z||000013||Eric||01-15-2000||10:35 AM||||Batboy, when I got my heads reworked I asked my machinist what he though about putting a larger exhaust valve it and he said there really wasn't enough room to get a big enough valve in there to achieve any real results.  I think he just didn't want to devote the time to doing it, but I'm still unsure of the potential gains.  Weren't the heads off Camaros equipted with larger exaust valves?  What if Shaun was to aquire a set of those for his rebuild???<P>Hey Shaun, I just signed myself up for the Mid Ohio thing this summer, so I guess I'll be seeing the BEAST there, huh?  If your interested in meeting a couple times in Norwalk to drag race, I'm all for it.  I don't know quite where it is, but Toledo is kind of a long haul for me.  Maybe we could meet once a Norwalk and once a National Trails.  There are a few bugs I have to get worked out of my car, but I'm willing to make the first trek up there just as soon as the track opens and you're free.||||reg||1
Z||000014||batboy||01-15-2000||03:09 PM||||Eric, I personally don't know if you can stuff the bigger exhaust valve in there or not, but was told by several different people at different times that the HO Fiero head casting is basically the same as those HO Camaro heads and that it can be done.<P>However, like you mentioned, picking up the right heads to begin with is probably the best and safest way to do it. Regardless, the pocket porting really does work. Good for maybe an extra 10 ponies and probably much more with nitrous because of the better breathing at top end.<P>You have to use a hi-po cam (like what you're using) and run headers to benefit fully from the increased head flow. No, it's not cheap, but scratching out reliable horsepower never is cheap.||||reg||1
Z||000015||Cooter||01-15-2000||06:27 PM||||I have a set of heads (from FieroDave) at the machine shop right now being outfitted with 1.8 intake and 1.5 exhaust valves. A 1.6 exhaust valve will almost fit, but clearance on the outer edge is too tight. He is doing some creative machining to a 283 intake valve and standard GM style exhaust. They will be the swirl polished, undercut stainless valves. I dont know how much more power this will make, but the guy is going to make me a good deal- he needs me to fix his shop's computer! BTW these heads will go on a 3.1 with the Compucam #2030 sometime in the future.||||reg||1
Z||000016||batboy||01-15-2000||07:54 PM||||Cooter, that sounds sweet! Power is made or lost in the heads. Ok, so now we know for sure that a 1.5" exhaust valve will fit but not a 1.6" valve. Unless, of course, you get those HO 3.4 Camaro heads to begin with. I'm going to start looking for a pair of those Camaro heads at swap meets and boneyards, see if I can find a bargain.||||reg||1
Z||000017||Shaun41178||01-15-2000||11:34 PM||||I have an old update for those that think the camaro heads come with 1.6.  Well I have been told that it is a big farce put on by GM  I guy I know and have talked to said he has 3.4 heads, in fact he has a whole 3.4 froma carmaor/firebird and they have the same exhaust valves as a Fiero.  No such thing as 1.6 exhaust valves in our heads.  Yes I know I was bummed about it too.  GM states it in their parts book but I have done further digging and found the truth.<P>Batboy: can you do me a favor.  I was hoping you oculd plug in my engine information and see what power I got out of it.  I am guessing you plugged in some info from a 1.5 rocker to a 1.6 rocker.  If you could give me  the info about what it said I had with 1.5 rocekrs that would be cool.  You can also plug this into your equation:<BR>1)gasket matched heads/intake- doubt this will give any improvement but if there is not a provision for it then don't worry about it.  <BR>2) mild Pocket port<BR>3)ported exhaust manifolds-headers in the future so youcould throw those in to contrast and compare.<BR>4)underdrive pulley<BR>5)2.5 cat-back with gutted cat and flowmaster muffler.<BR>6)removed air box and using straight pipe with K&N cone filter<BR>7)stainless valves with undercut stems<P>I am using a complete GM valvetrain and matching springs that go with my cam.<BR>If you could plug those in and see what I get that would be great.  Oh yea 9-1 compression.  If there aren't provisions for some of those mods then dont worry about it but if you could plug in as much as possible I would appreciate and let me know what the hp and torque are.  also if you want to add like a 50 shot of nitrous and see what I get that would be great too.  Thanks<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000018||batboy||01-16-2000||01:24 AM||||I have no way to figure in the air box or the pulley. The gasket matching is normally considered part of the pocket porting. My exhaust choices are stock, high performance manifolds with mufflers, small tube headers with mufflers, etc. I figured the HP with mufflers would be close to your present set-up. This program runs about 5-10 HP more optimistic than real dyno tests. Figure approximately 15% to 20% less for net HP at the wheels. Anyway, with the ported exhaust manifolds: 192 HP @ 5500 rpm and 207 TQ @ 4000. With 50 shot nitrous: 242 HP @ 5500 and 289 TQ @ 3000. With headers: 217 HP @5500 and 226 TQ @4500. With headers and laughing gas: 267 HP @5500 and 299 TQ @ 3000. This is all with 1.5:1 ratio rockers. Remember, these are just "ballpark" figures. The headers figures are probably higher than reality, because you can't buy true tube headers for Fieros. It'll be interesting to see what your combo ends up really dynoing at.||||reg||1
Z||000019||1FST2M6||01-17-2000||08:43 AM||||I can pull to well over 110psi on tthe fuel rail at the schrader valve. I've replaced the in tank fuel pump with one from Summit (catalog) its their HO 88-91 IROC replacement, along with replacing that i pulled the baffle set out of the bottom of the pump (not the tank the pump) for those of you who have not opened open up a fiero gas tank there is a monolith of plastic right at the end of the fuel pump made for stableizing out the fuel so it doesn't cavitate well that restricts the flow... thus less fuel... The stillen Fuel pressure riser is rated to 135psi peak but will hold 100 all day long. That fuel pressure riser is hooked to my NOS progressive controller which throws the factory computer (with ADS chip)in to closed loop (not O2 closed loop but more like a limp home mode) so that my crane HI-6s and the NOS controller pretty much run the show when the nitrous is on. With the exception of spark curve. I have  changed the bypass jetting which helps the HP number but seems to cut my bottom end torque...  Still trying to figure out how i can have the best of both worlds but not really getting anywhere.  As for my Ignition set up. Cranes Hi6s, Custom wires (Magnecore wires, MSD HI-temp boots) PS-92 Coil, MSD ignition control module, NAPA cap and NGK (UR5) spark plugs and ADS (strip chip) reprogrammed by Custom Network Motors for better fuel delivery under a given load from 2200-5100. The guys at CNM made the car run on the bigger injectors. She runs fine when not on the bottle but i only get about 14mpg on the highway and I can blast through a little less than a 1/4 of a tank of gas on one 1/4mile drag run. I just had the cars emisions tested, she passed everthing by Oxides of Nitrogen which is caused by (malfuncting EGR, and messed up spark curve) haven't fixed it yet. and Batboy, the number i found with the air box replacement was 4.1hp and 5.3lb-ft at the wheels. But that was also done at the same time as my throttle coolant bypass. (measured on a Dynojet Dyno model 248c) I'm off the go bug NOS now and see what i should do about my bypass jet... thanks <P><p>[This message has been edited by 1FST2M6 (edited 01-17-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000020||Eric||01-17-2000||06:49 PM||||Thanks for sending me those specs, 1FST2M6.  I don't think I want to ever get as serious as you with the nitrous.  This thing has got to be my daily driver and with such a small fuel tank, 14 miles to the gallon would barely get me to the next gas station out where I live.  But your car definatly sounds fast.  I couldn't imagine owning a car that 0-60's in 4.8 seconds.  My car is an 88GT with a "stroked" 3.1 motor with less than 2,000 miles on it.  I just installed a MSD blaster coil (to help the top end out a little) and the Autometer air/fuel gauge.  I only fired up the car for a few minutes to read the gauge.  It started off showing two yellow bars at idle then when I reved it, the little LEDs started blinking all over the place.  I hope it stops that nonsense.  I don't want there to be a lazer light show on my dash when I'm using every bit of concentration I have just to get me down these lonesome and dark country roads that are so popular around here.  I checked my fuel pressure and it was reading about 49psi.  I guess I could go a little higher if I needed to, but it would be nice to run a nitrous setup where I could still achieve some good gas miliage.  ||||reg||1
Z||000021||Cooter||01-17-2000||08:53 PM||||eric,<BR>In the "for what it's worth department", I added the analog Air/Fuel meter and it is never steady in the "ideal" range. Instead, the mixture swings from rich to lean constantly. Only under full throttle (and the sensor is bypassed) does it stay in the rich side of the gauge.||||reg||1
Z||000022||1FST2M6||01-18-2000||09:51 AM||||Get used to the light show! The nature of a fuel injected car is to toss the injection system from rich-lean-rich-lean... thats just to keep it at a steady rpm.. or an idle. You might want to take the light out of it, so at night all that it going on is the LEDs flashing and not the LEDs and the bright housing. Thats what i ended up doing. Yours will read in to the green i hope under any kind of decent throttle input! RIGHT! if not have you need the injectors cleaned! If you start to notice that the "laser show" is slowing down you might want to replace you O2 sensor... I have to replace mine every other bottle of nitrous. Glad autozone has a lifetime warranty... a new O2 every month... that would be expensive! I had a Haltech, Halmeter with injector load meter but took it out cause I'll NEVER use my bigger injectors to their full ability. If you think your straining your injectors you might want to see how bad with one of these units. You can get those from Racer Wholesale in Atlanta and somewhere in Illinois. A 3.1 hunh.. what kind of pistons? stock or forged? You'll need to be careful with anything other than Factory cast or a good forged piston. I killed my old 79 Corvette with Keith Black Hypereutetic pistons. The nitrous being an oxidizer bonded with the aluminum and didn't even last one full shot. And all the guys at NOS said was "you didn't read the instrustions" well in the instrustion of my kit and on thier web page they say "No Aluminum or hyper* piston are to be used with our kits" Your head will be fine and if you had the cool GM aluminum 2.8+ block it would be fine too but not the pistons..  check thier web page for more info.  <A HREF="http://www.nosnitrous.com" TARGET=_blank>www.nosnitrous.com</A>   Just filled up this morning.. got 91 miles out of the last tank of gas... And fit 8.1 gallons at the tune of $11.  11.23- mpg.  glad i live close to work! the 4 mile drive isn't that bad. I've got a pic of the new clutch i'll send ya too...||||reg||1
Z||000023||Eric||01-18-2000||05:05 PM||||Yeah, I've got the hyperutectic pistons.  This really sticks!  I was all set to toss a little blue bottle in my trunk and hit the streets.  Oh well, what's done is done.  I guess I should start looking at forced induction again.  It'll be a lot bigger headache, but at least it won't melt my pistons down.  Why do the stock cast units hold up better?  Maybe because they aren't a special alloy.  Where do you get non aluminum pistons anyway?  What are they made of titanium or carbon fiber?  Just kidding.  I knew I was being cheap when I didn't opt for the $1000 a set JE forged pistons.  So I guess this is where I end up.  <P>I see what you guys mean about the lasar light show.  I try to calm it down a little bit, but maybe it would be a cool thing for a passenger to fixate on.<P>Shaun, I hope you're reading this.  I'd hate for you engine to experience meltdown on it's first bottle of nitrous.  Maybe 1FST2M6 has a source on pistons.||||reg||1
Z||000024||Cooter||01-18-2000||07:13 PM||||I thought all modern pistons were some type of aluminum. ||||reg||1
Z||000025||1FST2M6||01-18-2000||08:01 PM||||Arias and TRW both make a good forged Piston that will NOT adversly react with nitrous. I got mine from Summit Racing. For about $680 for the set. Their in another block waiting for the factory one to die... I've spoken with shaun before and he is aware of the piston issue. Both arias,TRW-Federal Moguel (sp) and NOS have a more in depth explination of why this happens and why not to run them tha i can give other than my own experiance. As for the "...cast factory units their are only trace amounts of aluminum in them so there is no real issue with them along with many other vehicle the pistons are made to be cheap to manufacture and last a long time aluminum is neither of these. A decent mild steel is usually utilized in these cases..." (According to the GM Performance book at Carl Black Pontiac here in Kennesaw, Ga.)   <p>[This message has been edited by 1FST2M6 (edited 01-18-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000026||Cooter||01-18-2000||09:14 PM|||| <A HREF="http://www.jp.com.au/Made.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.jp.com.au/Made.html</A> <BR>Sorry, but according to JP Pistons: "The material used is a high silicon aluminium. The grade used is A504 which contains between 13-15% silicon"<BR>According to Keith Black: <A HREF="http://www.kb-silvolite.com/page02.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.kb-silvolite.com/page02.htm</A>  <A HREF="http://www.kb-silvolite.com/page14.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.kb-silvolite.com/page14.htm</A> <BR>"We are currently testing some pistons specifically designed for Nitrous use. Current "off the shelf" pistons have been successfully run with a 500 HP nitrous kit combined with a Dr. Jacob's nitrous control system."-- This is a quote from Keith Black on the use of their KB automotive pistons.<BR>According to that nos link, they only recommend forged pistons for the cheater and more powerful systems.<p>[This message has been edited by Cooter (edited 01-18-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000027||Eric||01-18-2000||09:28 PM||||I didn't think pistons were made of anything except aluminum.  So hypers are an aluminum alloy and don't hold together well in the presence of an oxidizer and forged are pure forged aluminum.  Right?  It's starting to make sense.  So these pistons we have in our cars are not likely to see as many miles as cast pistons?  Not that I really expect them to anyhow, but I thought hyperutectics were an upgrade built to withstand more stress.  Is this still true?  Is the problem a chemical reaction that breaks down the metal in the piston?  I seem to have a lot of questions for someone whose given up on nitrous.<P>So what's the deal with your car, 1FST2M6?  Were you trying to sell it?  I could only imagine what it would be like trying to sell my car now.  You couldn't expect someone else to appreciate how much time you spent under the thing turning wrenches and what not.  I really hope you keep it or at least stay in the Fiero game.  It really helps having feedback from people who have been there and done that.||||reg||1
Z||000028||Terrybogin||01-18-2000||09:51 PM||||I just stumbled into this thread. 1FST2M6, I think you are full of... well maybe sniffed too much nitros. 55lb/hr injectors at 90+ psi on a 2.8 v6? Yeah, right, whatever. My son's 400+ HP BBC runs four 85lb/hr injectors at 16 psi and the duty cycle is still way low. Do the math 4*85 and 6*55 (ratings for the injectors) and tell me that you are for really serious. ||||reg||1
Z||000029||1FST2M6||01-19-2000||11:14 AM||||I am running 55lb-hr injectors purchased them from RC Engineering in Calif. I found out the piston issue when my 79 Corvette 383 4spd (feed buy a vacuum secondary Holley 750)died just as i pushed the button, (The nitrous kit was installed by a local speed shop, not myself (supoed to be only a 125hp shot)) And the guys at NOS told me why it died and what had happen to the pistons. After a complete tear down what they told me was correct, dime to quarter size holes in the pistons (3 of them) If you like more information or other proof of my findings and other upgrades don't hesitate to contact the shop of Engineered Performance Inc.  <A HREF="http://www.engineered.net" TARGET=_blank>www.engineered.net</A>  They along with Custom Network Motors ( AOL keyword TurboCNM (builders of the worlds only Twin Turbo Civic)) transformed the car to what it is today. With my check book and their knowledge and a little trial and error we turned my low 16 sec dog into the mid 13 sec stop light sleeper she is now. I've had many people come look at the car and only 3 drive it. all of which say its too much car or it has too many mods for thier liking. What they fail to realize is all the major mods were performed by an ASE certified Master Tech and I have the reciepts to prove it. Sorry if I sound defensive but i've spent SO much time and money on this jewel and All i really get is grief.. Belive what you want and think what you like i'm just letting you know what my problems have been and hoping not to have anyone else have these same costly mistakes. My new Arias are forged mild steel. ... Ladies and Gentlemen after reviewing of my invoice from RC Engineering. The injectors I am running are constant 36# and with peak hold of 55#. I apollogize for misleading you. On the KB page "The book titled "Nitrous Oxide Injection" by David Vizard, published by S-A Design " I have that book it has a blown/nitroused V8 on the cover with a blue outline and in this book it says the same thing i am, about the piston issue. It cost me $15 at Barnes and Nobel<BR><p>[This message has been edited by 1FST2M6 (edited 01-19-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000030||batboy||01-19-2000||02:51 PM||||Ti the best of my knowledge, all normal high performance pistons are made of some various type of aluminum alloy. The Arias Racing Piston website has a broken link and I can't access it.<BR> <A HREF="http://www.ariaspistons.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.ariaspistons.com</A> <P>Last I heard though, Arias pistons were either forged from 2618 or 4032 billet bar stock aluminum alloy. Never heard of steel pistons. Are you sure you heard right? Maybe somebody's trying to blow smoke up your butt.||||reg||1
Z||000031||1FST2M6||01-19-2000||03:06 PM||||nope, have the recipt right here, Mild Steel forging. I have the same broken link... ||||reg||1
Z||000032||1FST2M6||01-19-2000||03:13 PM||||Do none of you have an old set of factory pistons laying around? Rinse them off in the kitchen sink, DON'T dry them and set them on the back porch for 24 hrs. ... POOF surface rust! Aluminum doesn't rust it oxidizes, (white and powdery not Red and gritty) Have about 2 factory sets rusting in my garage! ( have 9)<p>[This message has been edited by 1FST2M6 (edited 01-19-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000033||Cooter||01-19-2000||03:24 PM||||Okay, a little knowledge learned from building championship winning kart engines. An aluminum bore has to have a chrome plated piston because the un-plated piston will gall and seize in the aluminum bore. Many moons ago when pistons were made of steel, they were coated so they would not stick in the bore of a cast iron engine. If you have steel pistons, then you need an aluminum bore or an aluminum coated steel. If what you say is true about the nitrous eating the aluminum, then your aluminum coated pistons would suffer the same fate as the others. <BR>If you are paying someone to do all this work, then Batboy may be right. Someone is puffing smoke up your butt. I used to work at a large area dealership, I am ICAR and ASE certified have a very useless degree in automotive engineering with an add on certification in small engines. I think I know a little about this stuff.||||reg||1
Z||000034||1FST2M6||01-19-2000||04:24 PM||||I'm not sure about the coatings and such. This is what tiny bit I do know. From reading stuff and trashing my old 383. Not to use Hyper* or aluminum pistons on a nitrous fed engine. NOS doesn't recommend it and neither does The book titled "Nitrous Oxide Injection" by David Vizard, published by S-A Design. Cause i read that before i even started on the nitrous set up for my fiero. In addition to ".. you can never have to much gas in a nitrous fed engine..." all they really say is "...becareful on your selection of pistons" My set are a mild steel forging not sure about any coating.. Their may be! I'm just not aware of it. I do know that when the tech people at NOS found out that i was running KB Hyper* slugs all they said was "...you didn't read all the instructions did you..." And that they were right on the money with the holes in my pistons. Sorry for taking up so many peoples time. It just cost me a chunck of change cause ".. I didn't read all the instructions..." So i got all the instrustions i could find before i had a chance to kill the Fiero. And hope to aid others. ||||reg||1
Z||000035||batboy||01-19-2000||05:35 PM||||I don't mean to sound like I'm giving anyone a hard time, but this ol' hotrodder from way back is having trouble believing that there is any such critter as a steel forged racing piston. They would be much heavier which goes against the grain and I can only imagine the amount of cylinder scuffing caused from the piston skirts.<P>Ok, I called my local speed shop. The looked up in their Arias catalog and there are <B>NO</B> steel pistons made by Arias. There is a heavy duty aluminum forged "B-series" racing piston recommended for forced induction or nitrous oxide injected high performance engines. The description says it comes with steel pins which is where the confusion might be coming from, but the actual piston is a type of aluminum alloy.||||reg||1
Z||000036||Eric||01-19-2000||05:41 PM||||I did see rust on my factory pistons after I cleaned them off, but I'm not convinced it was really rust.  I think the pistons were made out of aluminum for the most part with other various materials throughout them.  The rust could be accounted for by the trace amounts of iron in the piston (the rust wasn't even the color of rust).  Also the pistons were way too light to be steel.  1FST2M6, I don't want you to take this the wrong way, but Steel Pistons???  I still appreciate your advice about not using N2O with hyper pistons and your knowledge concerning other topics, however, I don't think this makes any sense at all.||||reg||1
Z||000037||DRH||01-19-2000||05:49 PM||||I have never heard of a mild steel piston either (my knowledge of hi perf stuff is dated at best though). Anybody have a magnet and the piston in question?<P>1fst2m6, no offense. really great for you to share your experience. don't let the skeptics discourage you from sharing in the future.<p>[This message has been edited by DRH (edited 01-19-2000).]||||reg||1
