Z||000039||Terrybogin||01-19-2000||08:28 PM||||Thanks for sharing your info with us. however, the only thing worse than no information is misinformation. Sorry, but very little in your post lends itself to credible information: 55lb/hr injectors that turned out to be 36lb/hr<BR>90+ psi fuel pressure<BR>Only 164 HP with approx 130 HP of No2 going through it. . . <BR>I have said enough, so I be be quiet now.||||reg||1
Z||000040||batboy||01-19-2000||10:55 PM||||I got the Arias website to work now, but cannot find much info on the site, certainly no info about steel pistons.<P>One thing is for sure, I've always said that you should use good forged pistons with forced induction and/or nitrous. I have used hyper pistons in a normally aspirated high performance Chevy 350 engine and it worked out great.||||reg||1
Z||000041||1FST2M6||01-20-2000||08:28 AM||||OK Thanks guys. I'm just purging what i've done and what i've read. I thank you all for your insight and I too have called Summit Racing and tech people at Arias. Cause everything i have in front of me. "How to tune your nitrous" and "Nitrous Oxide Injection" say the same thing and I may be incorrect. I'm confident that the bigger RC injectors are the only thing keeping my car from thrashing itself in to little bits! Yes my car only makes 164HP, but 240+lb-ft of torque at the wheels in 4th gear at 1500rpm!! Have the dyno results to show. Thats front end lifting torque! And i've gone through 13 or 14 bottles now! on a motor with 90,000+ miles on her and the only thing internally changed are the valves and the valve stem seals. I appreciate you guys calling me on my piston issue and I've contacted the makers of my set to find out what is supose to be going on. I hope none of you think any less of me for my incorrect fodder. I apollogize for any inconveniance I've cause any of you. Thanks for your assistance.  ||||reg||1
